#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:65
msgid "`trunc`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:67
msgid ""
"Truncates a date or timestamp to a given unit. The unit is written as a "
"string literal, unlike `date.diff`, which takes it as a bare keyword; which "
"units are accepted follows the target database. The unit has to be known at "
"compile time, so a column or expression in its place is an error."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:72
msgid ""
"```prql\n"
"prql target:sql.postgres\n"
"\n"
"from events\n"
"select (event_time | date.trunc \"day\")\n"
"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:80
msgid ""
"Most dialects take the unit as a quoted first argument, but MSSQL and "
"BigQuery each use their own form:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:85 src/reference/stdlib/date.md:86
#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:87 src/reference/stdlib/date.md:88
#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:89
msgid "`DATE_TRUNC('day', event_time)`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:86 src/reference/stdlib/date.md:118
msgid "DuckDB"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:87
msgid "Postgres"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:90 src/reference/stdlib/date.md:123
msgid "Snowflake"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:90
msgid "`DATE_TRUNC('day', \"event_time\")`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:91 src/reference/stdlib/date.md:119
msgid "MSSQL"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:91
msgid "`DATETRUNC(day, event_time)`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:92
msgid "`DATE_TRUNC(event_time, DAY)`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:95
msgid ""
"\\[!NOTE\\] SQLite has no date-truncation function, so `date.trunc` raises "
"an error when compiling for that dialect."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:99
msgid "`diff`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:101
msgid ""
"Returns `end` minus `start`, measured in `unit` — so `date.diff unit start "
"end` is positive when `end` is later. The unit is written as a bare keyword, "
"unlike `date.trunc`, which takes it as a string; which units are accepted "
"follows the target database."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:111
msgid ""
"`date.diff` compiles to the target database's own date-difference function, "
"so the exact rounding behavior — whether a partial unit counts — follows "
"that database rather than being normalized by PRQL:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:117 src/reference/stdlib/date.md:119
msgid "`DATEDIFF(day, start_date, end_date)`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:118
msgid "`datediff('day', start_date, end_date)`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:120
msgid "`TIMESTAMPDIFF(day, start_date, end_date)`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:121
msgid "`dateDiff('day', start_date, end_date)`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:122
msgid "`DATE_DIFF(end_date, start_date, day)`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:123
msgid "`DATEDIFF(day, \"start_date\", \"end_date\")`"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:126
msgid ""
"\\[!NOTE\\] Postgres and SQLite have no `DATEDIFF` equivalent, so `date."
"diff` raises an error when compiling for those dialects."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:130
msgid "Date & time format specifiers"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/stdlib/date.md:132
msgid ""
"PRQL specifiers for date and time formatting is a subset of specifiers used "
"by [`chrono`](https://docs.rs/chrono/latest/chrono/format/strftime/index."
"html)."
msgstr ""

